The more serious difficulty in trying to apply consistently a Weberian conception of government – and the one of much more importance for this book – is illustrated by the following example.
Suppose that for some purpose under consideration everyone agrees that some small isolated community is the relevant territory of analysis.
Suppose further that every person in this community has explicitly and voluntarily agreed that a single third party's decisions shall govern them, say the community elder's, enforced with threats of violence exclusively by his command.
Is the elder this community has decided shall govern it a government?
He has a monopoly on social rule creation and enforcement in the territory in question.
A Weberian conception, then, would seem to suggest he is.